    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2016637284867584273",
      "url": "https://x.com/r00ts21/status/2016637284867584273",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/r00ts21/status/2016637284867584273",
      "text": "How does USAT benefit Strike more importantly, how does Strike benefit USAT? What's $XXI's role in all this?\n\nTo understand the \"win-win\" between Tether and Strike, you have to look past the simple \"sending a coin\" utility and focus on network effects, compliance, and settlement finality.\n\nWhile you can send Tether (USDT/USAT) directly on blockchains like Tron or Solana, those are essentially \"siloed\" ecosystems. The combination of Tether + Strike (via Bitcoin Layer 2) creates a global liquidity super-highway that makes traditional blockchains look like local roads.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why this partnership is a \"gorilla-tier\" move for the new monetary system:\n\n1. The \"Network of Networks\" (Interoperability)\nThe Problem with Native Blockchain Transfers: If you send USDT on Tron, the recipient must have a Tron wallet and TRX for gas. If they are in a remote part of the Philippines or Ghana, they then have to find an exchange to turn that Tron-USDT into local Pesos or Cedis.\n\nThe Strike/L2 Win: Strike uses the Lightning Network (L2) as a universal translator. Strike can take USAT/USDT in the US, \"teleport\" the value across the Lightning Network as Bitcoin (instantly and for fractions of a penny), and deliver it to a recipient's bank account in 65+ countries as local fiat currency. * The Value: Strike isn't just a wallet; it's a remittance and FX engine that uses L2 to bypass the 3-5% fees and 3-day delays of the SWIFT system.\n\n2. Solving the \"Gas\" and \"Complexity\" Barrier\nDirect Blockchain: To use USDT on Ethereum or Solana, a non-technical user has to manage \"gas fees\" (ETH, SOL, or TRX). This is a massive friction point for mass adoption.\n\nThe Strike/L2 Win: Strike abstracts the blockchain away. The user sees \"Dollars\" (USAT/USDT). When they hit send, the \"gas\" (routing fees) is handled by the Lightning nodes (potentially managed by $XXI).\n\nThe Value: It provides the user experience of Venmo with the settlement power of a global blockchain.\n\n3. Regulatory & Tax Efficiency (The \"Synthetic\" Dollar)\nThe Scenario: Moving Bitcoin can trigger a capital gains tax event.\n\nThe Win: By using USAT (a regulated, US-compliant stablecoin) as the entry and exit point, and only using the Bitcoin L2 as the \"hidden\" transport layer, Strike allows institutions to move value globally without the tax headaches of holding or \"trading\" Bitcoin. You get the speed of Bitcoin without the volatility or tax risk of Bitcoin.\n\n4. Tactical Advantage for $XXI and Tether\nTether's Win: Tether is the \"fuel\" (the liquidity). By integrating with Strike, Tether ensures its stablecoins remain the most liquid and useful in the world, preventing them from being sidelined by CBDCs or bank-issued tokens.\n\n$XXI's Win: As the holder of the massive BTC reserve, $XXI acts as the \"Liquidity Provider.\" They provide the BTC that sits in the Lightning channels to make sure Strike's \"Send Globally\" feature never runs out of capacity.",
